# Editors and pagers, each opened on a real file.

shot    elvrec
for     elvrec elvprsv vi.elvis input.elvis
cap     elvis's crash-recovery pair: elvprsv preserves an interrupted
cap     session, elvrec brings it back.  Nothing has been preserved on
cap     this disk, so elvrec lists nothing at all and only names the file
cap     when you ask for one by name.  `exrecover' is the same job for the
cap     other vi here, and says what it wants.
run     elvrec
wait    6
run     elvrec /dd/tmp/none
wait    6
run     exrecover
wait    6
run     elvprsv /dd/tmp/none
wait    6

shot    emacs
for     emacs emacs.mm1 umacs
cap     MicroEmacs 4.00 editing a file from the disk, with its own mode
cap     line at the foot.
run     emacs /dd/SYS/motd
wait    10
quit    \030\003

shot    hexedit
for     hexed hexedit
cap     The hex editor cannot start: it writes its work file to /r0, the RAM
cap     disk os9exec has no way to provide, and says so.  Seventeen programs
cap     here reach for /r0; DOC/README-RUNNING lists them.  `beav' is the
cap     binary editor that works.  `hexedit' below is a different program
cap     with a similar name -- HEXPERT V2.4, which identifies itself under
cap     -v and then gets no further: -d says `file not accessible' for a
cap     file that exists and is readable, and the bare form prints the
cap     terminal type and exits.
run     hexed /dd/CMDS/today
wait    6
quit    q
run     hexedit -v /dd/CMDS/today
wait    6
run     hexedit -d /dd/CMDS/today
wait    6
run     hexedit /dd/CMDS/today
wait    8

shot    pbyte
for     pbyte chbase
cap     pbyte patches bytes in a file at a hex offset, and chbase changes a
cap     module's base -- the two smallest binary editors here.
run     pbyte
wait    8
run     chbase
wait    8

shot    sed
for     sed sed_1.06 psc
cap     sed doing what a stream editor does, twice over, on the disk's own
cap     documentation.
run     head -n 8 /dd/SYS/holidays > /dd/tmp/hol.txt
wait    6
run     sed "s/Day/DAY/g" /dd/tmp/hol.txt
wait    8


shot    vi
for     vi view
cap     The EFFO vi -- its source in SRC/effo_vi is the Berkeley ex source.
cap     It opens the file and paints TWO LINES of it: the first line and a
cap     half-drawn rule, and no more however long you wait or wherever you
cap     move.  Three others here fill the screen properly: `elvis',
cap     `vi_nocio' and REBUILT/VI, each with its own card.
run     vi /dd/DOC/README-CIO
wait    20
send    \033
wait    3
send    1G
wait    10
quit    \033:q!\r

shot    elvis
for     elvis vi.elvis input.elvis ctags.elvis
cap     elvis, Steve Kirkendall's vi -- the one on this disk that paints a
cap     full screen.  Opened on the collection's own README-CIO, twenty-two
cap     lines of it at once, exactly as vi should.
run     elvis /dd/DOC/README-CIO
wait    18
quit    \033:q!\r

shot    pvic
for     VI
cap     PVIC, the Portable VI Clone, in CMDS/REBUILT because the name `vi'
cap     was already taken by the EFFO one.  It is a working vi: tildes down
cap     the left, the file painted a screenful at a time, and o-i-escape-:wq
cap     writes the line you typed.  Asked for by path, since it is not on
cap     the login PATH.
run     /dd/CMDS/REBUILT/VI /dd/DOC/README-CIO
wait    18
quit    \033:q!\r

